.TH UNIQ 1 "September 19, 2026" maux
.SH NAME
uniq \- report repeated lines
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B uniq
.RB [ \-cdu ]
.RB [ \-f " fields" ]
.RB [ \-s " chars" ]
.RI [ input " [" output ]]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Uniq
reads the input file comparing adjacent lines.
In the normal case, the second and succeeding copies of repeated lines
are removed; the remainder is written on the output file.
Note that repeated lines must be adjacent in order to be found;
see sort(1).
Input and output default to standard input and standard output.
.TP
.B \-u
Print just the lines that are not repeated.
.TP
.B \-d
Print one copy of just the repeated lines.
.TP
.B \-c
Precede each selected line by its repeat count.
.TP
.B \-f fields
Ignore this many initial fields in comparisons.
A field is a string of non-space, non-tab characters with preceding blanks.
.TP
.B \-s chars
Ignore this many further characters. Fields are skipped before characters.
.PP
The full original line is printed regardless of comparison skips.
The -d and -u options are mutually exclusive.
.SH FILES
/usr/man/UNIQ  this page
.SH "SEE ALSO"
sort(1), comm(1), licenses(1)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
Exit status is 0 on success, 1 for file I/O failures, 2 for usage errors.
.SH BUGS
Lines longer than 255 bytes are treated as several logical lines. An existing longer output file is not shortened; choose a new output file. Do not use the input as the output.
